Z||000030||mbramble||12-15-2000||07:40 PM||||Well, that's the problem isn't it??  Why should either candidate get a vote that wasn't clearly cast for him??  That was the concern in the 5-4 ruling of the U.S. Supreme Court.  In fact, it was the concern 7-2 of the Court.  The difference is that 2 of the 7 thought that you cold create a standard and continue counting until the 18th whereas 5 of 7 ruled that to enjoy the safe harbor that the Florida Legislature desired when they wrote the election code, and that the Florida Supreme Court referenced in it's rulings, the counting had to be finished by the 12th.<P>It's one thing to spout that all votes should be counted, it's another to go about it properly with a standard on what should be considered a vote and impartial counters who have been trained to count using the standard.  The state of Florida had no such provisions in effect on election day.  It doesn't matter if Texas, or Michigan, or every other state in the Union did, Florida didn't. <P>You can't, as the FSS tried, just say "go and count and determine the will of the voter" with no guidance.  As has been quoted several times during this whole mess, Joseph Stalin said "Voters decide nothing; people who count votes decide everything."  <P>You have to be sure that people who are doing the counting are actually counting votes, not casting them.||||reg||1
